{
 "cells": [
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "46cc757a-f01b-4f7c-8c6a-94fb10218e9a",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    " What is “weight decay” used for in training neural networks? A) To regularize the model and prevent \n",
    "overfitting\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "Weight decay is a regularization technique used during neural network training to prevent overfitting by discouraging large weights. It works by\n",
    "penalizing the magnitude of the weights in the loss function.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "    Loss = error between true output and predicted output\n",
    "\n",
    "Total Loss = Original Loss + lambda × (sum of all weights squared)\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "- lambda\" is a small number that controls how strong the penalty is.\n",
    "- \"sum of all weights squared\" means you're adding up the square of every weight in the model.\n",
    "\n",
    "🧠 Why This Helps\n",
    "- If weights get too large, the penalty increases.\n",
    "- So the optimizer prefers smaller weights — which usually means simpler models.\n",
    "- Simpler models tend to generalize better and avoid overfitting.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "hc \n",
    "This encourages the optimizer to shrink weights toward zero, but not exactly zero — preserving useful signal while reducing complexity.\n",
    "\n",
    "🧭 Geometric Intuition\n",
    "Imagine the weight space as a high-dimensional landscape. Without regularization, the optimizer might find sharp valleys (overfit solutions). \n",
    "Weight decay smooths the landscape, nudging the solution toward flatter regions with smaller weights — which generalize better.\n",
    "\n",
    "🛠️ Implementation in Practice\n",
    "In most frameworks (like PyTorch or TensorFlow), weight decay is applied during optimization:\n",
    "optimizer = torch.optim.Adam(model.parameters(), lr=0.001, weight_decay=1e-5)\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "This automatically adds the L2 penalty during gradient updates.\n",
    "\n",
    "✅ Why It Helps\n",
    "- Reduces overfitting by limiting model complexity\n",
    "- Improves generalization to unseen data\n",
    "- Stabilizes training by avoiding extreme weight values\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "Original Loss Function\n",
    "L_original = Loss(y_true, y_pred)\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "➕ With Weight Decay (L2 Regularization)\n",
    "L_total = L_original + lambda × sum_i(w_i²)\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "- w_i are the individual weights in the model\n",
    "- lambda is the regularization strength (weight decay coefficient)\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "d9088634-33fb-4813-b2b7-ab2a6b539e81",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "L2 Regularization: Layer-Level vs Optimizer-Level\n",
    "\n",
    "Aspect                     | Layer-Level Penalty                          | Optimizer-Level Penalty (`weight_decay`)\n",
    "---------------------------|----------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------\n",
    "Where it's defined         | Inside each layer (e.g., `kernel_regularizer`) | In the optimizer (e.g., `Adam(..., weight_decay=...)`)\n",
    "Applies to                 | Specific layer weights only                  | All model parameters passed to optimizer\n",
    "Frameworks                 | TensorFlow/Keras                             | PyTorch, TensorFlow (from v2.9+)\n",
    "Control granularity        | Fine-grained (per-layer control)             | Coarse-grained (global control)\n",
    "Penalty type               | Explicit L2 loss added to total loss         | Implicit L2 penalty during gradient update\n",
    "Bias regularization        | Optional (`bias_regularizer`)               | Usually applies to all parameters unless filtered\n",
    "Behavior during training   | Adds term to loss function                   | Modifies gradients directly during optimization\n",
    "\n",
    "Conclusion:\n",
    "✅ Layer-level penalties give fine control over which weights are regularized.\n",
    "✅ Optimizer-level `weight_decay` applies L2 globally and alters gradients directly.\n",
    "✅ Both achieve L2 regularization, but through different mechanisms.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "Layer-Level Regularization Behavior:\n",
    "\n",
    "What happens:\n",
    "- You define regularization (e.g., L1, L2) in a specific layer.\n",
    "- During training, the model adds the penalty term to the total loss **only for that layer’s weights**.\n",
    "- This penalty is computed and applied at **every batch and every epoch**.\n",
    "\n",
    "Example:\n",
    "Dense(64, activation='relu', kernel_regularizer=regularizers.l2(1e-4))\n",
    "\n",
    "✅ Only the weights of this Dense layer get penalized.\n",
    "❌ Other layers without `kernel_regularizer` are unaffected.\n",
    "\n",
    "Behind the scenes:\n",
    "- The regularization term is added to the loss function dynamically.\n",
    "- It’s tracked as part of the model’s `losses` list and summed with the primary loss (e.g., cross-entropy).\n",
    "\n",
    "Conclusion:\n",
    "✅ Yes, the model “remembers” and applies the penalty consistently to the specified layer throughout training."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "b80c9218-27d9-4a46-a97a-e58778f5da80",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "L1 = Lasso \n",
    "- L1 penalty adds the absolute value of weights to the loss:\n",
    "Total Loss = Original Loss + lambda × sum_i |w_i|\n",
    "- This creates a sparse model — many weights become exactly zero.\n",
    "- Great for feature selection: it automatically drops irrelevant features.\n",
    "\n",
    "✅ L2 = Ridge \n",
    "- L2 penalty adds the squared value of weights:\n",
    "Total Loss = Original Loss + lambda × sum_i w_i²\n",
    "- This shrinks weights smoothly but rarely to zero.\n",
    "- Good for stabilizing models and reducing overfitting without sparsity.\n",
    "\n",
    "🧭 Geometric Intuition\n",
    "- L1 (Lasso) constraint forms a diamond-shaped region → optimizer hits corners → zero weights.\n",
    "- L2 (Ridge) constraint forms a circular region → optimizer spreads weights evenly.\n",
    "\n",
    "If you'd like, I can show side-by-side plots of L1 vs L2 constraint regions or how they affect weight distributions. Want to visualize that?\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "Geometric Intuition\n",
    "- L1 (Lasso) constraint forms a diamond-shaped region → optimizer hits corners → zero weights.\n",
    "- L2 (Ridge) constraint forms a circular region → optimizer spreads weights evenly.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "## 🔍 L1 vs L2 Regularization\n",
    "\n",
    "| Feature            | L1 Regularization (Lasso)       | L2 Regularization (Ridge)       |\n",
    "|--------------------|----------------------------------|----------------------------------|\n",
    "| Penalty term       | lambda × sum_i |w_i|            | lambda × sum_i w_i²             |\n",
    "| Effect on weights  | Many become exactly zero         | All shrink, few go to zero      |\n",
    "| Model sparsity     | Sparse (feature selection)       | Dense (no feature elimination)  |\n",
    "| Geometry           | Diamond-shaped constraint region | Circular constraint region      |\n",
    "| Use case           | Feature selection, interpretability | Stability, smooth shrinkage  |\n",
    "| Optimization path  | Often hits axes (zero weights)   | Smooth descent, avoids zero     |\n",
    "\n",
    "## ✅ Summary\n",
    "\n",
    "- L1 (Lasso) → promotes sparsity, good for feature selection  \n",
    "- L2 (Ridge) → smooth shrinkage, good for stability and generalization  \n",
    "- Both reduce overfitting, but in different ways"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "97ec596f-69a4-4af8-ac69-3b6a6518cae0",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "## 🧠 What Is a Sparse Model?\n",
    "\n",
    "A **sparse model** is one where **many weights are exactly zero**.\n",
    "\n",
    "### ✅ Why This Happens\n",
    "- In L1 regularization (Lasso), the penalty encourages weights to shrink to zero.\n",
    "- This means the model **ignores** many input features — treating them as irrelevant.\n",
    "\n",
    "### 🔍 Sparse = Feature Selection\n",
    "- Only a **small subset of features** get non-zero weights.\n",
    "- The model automatically selects the most important features.\n",
    "- This leads to:\n",
    "  - Simpler models\n",
    "  - Faster inference\n",
    "  - Easier interpretation\n",
    "\n",
    "### 📊 Example\n",
    "Suppose you have 100 input features:\n",
    "- A dense model might use all 100 (non-zero weights).\n",
    "- A sparse model might use only 10 — the rest have zero weights.\n",
    "\n",
    "### 🧭 Summary\n",
    "- Sparse model = many zero weights\n",
    "- L1 regularization = promotes sparsity\n",
    "- Sparsity = automatic feature selection"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "286cde55-fe11-4ea4-9385-01324bae7cef",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "model.add(Dense(64, kernel_regularizer=regularizers.l1(0.0001)))  # L1 regularization\n",
    "l1_lambda = 0.0001\n",
    "l1_norm = sum(p.abs().sum() for p in model.parameters())\n",
    "loss = original_loss + l1_lambda * l1_norm"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "c9b9b37a-81e7-4ee7-b166-00815299c3a6",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": []
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "25e83d3d-ab6e-4fd9-9050-6c9cabb90879",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "Great observation, Rama — it looks like we’re “just adding a term to the loss,” but that extra term has a very concrete effect on how the **weights get updated during backpropagation**. Let’s break it down.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "## 🔑 Purpose of Weight Decay (L2 Regularization)\n",
    "- **Penalizes large weights**: The extra term \\(\\lambda \\sum_i w_i^2\\) grows when weights are large.  \n",
    "- **Encourages smaller weights**: Minimizing the total loss pushes weights toward smaller values.  \n",
    "- **Prevents overfitting**: Smaller weights mean the model is less sensitive to noise in the training data, improving generalization.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "## ⚙️ How It Affects Gradients\n",
    "During backpropagation, we compute gradients of the **total loss**:\n",
    "\n",
    "\\[\n",
    "L_{\\text{total}} = L_{\\text{original}} + \\lambda \\sum_i w_i^2\n",
    "\\]\n",
    "\n",
    "Gradient wrt a weight \\(w_j\\):\n",
    "\n",
    "\\[\n",
    "\\frac{\\partial L_{\\text{total}}}{\\partial w_j} = \\frac{\\partial L_{\\text{original}}}{\\partial w_j} + 2\\lambda w_j\n",
    "\\]\n",
    "\n",
    "👉 That extra \\(2\\lambda w_j\\) term is the “decay force” pulling weights toward zero.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "## 🔄 Update Rule with Weight Decay\n",
    "Standard gradient descent update:\n",
    "\n",
    "\\[\n",
    "w_j \\leftarrow w_j - \\eta \\cdot \\frac{\\partial L_{\\text{total}}}{\\partial w_j}\n",
    "\\]\n",
    "\n",
    "Expanding:\n",
    "\n",
    "\\[\n",
    "w_j \\leftarrow w_j - \\eta \\cdot \\frac{\\partial L_{\\text{original}}}{\\partial w_j} - \\eta \\cdot (2\\lambda w_j)\n",
    "\\]\n",
    "\n",
    "- The first term is the usual gradient from the data.  \n",
    "- The second term shrinks the weight proportionally to its size.  \n",
    "\n",
    "So every step, weights are nudged toward **smaller values** even if the data gradient is zero.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "## 🧾 Numeric Mini-Demo\n",
    "Suppose:\n",
    "- \\(w = 0.8\\), learning rate \\(\\eta = 0.1\\), \\(\\lambda = 0.01\\)  \n",
    "- Gradient from original loss: \\(\\frac{\\partial L_{\\text{original}}}{\\partial w} = 0.5\\)\n",
    "\n",
    "Then:\n",
    "\n",
    "\\[\n",
    "\\frac{\\partial L_{\\text{total}}}{\\partial w} = 0.5 + 2 \\cdot 0.01 \\cdot 0.8 = 0.516\n",
    "\\]\n",
    "\n",
    "Update:\n",
    "\n",
    "\\[\n",
    "w_{\\text{new}} = 0.8 - 0.1 \\cdot 0.516 = 0.7484\n",
    "\\]\n",
    "\n",
    "👉 Notice how the weight shrinks a little more than it would without regularization.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "## 🎯 Takeaway\n",
    "- Adding weight decay isn’t just “changing the loss.”  \n",
    "- It **changes the gradient** by adding a shrinkage term.  \n",
    "- This keeps weights small, reduces model complexity, and improves generalization.  \n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "Would you like me to show you a **copy‑paste Python snippet** that compares weight updates **with vs without L2 regularization** side‑by‑side, so you can see the shrinkage effect numerically?\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "141beca3-c274-4a60-ba4d-9014e7db04e7",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "Got it, Rama 👍 — here’s a copy‑paste ready Python snippet that walks through all the equations we discussed for L2 regularization (weight decay). It shows the original loss, the total loss with the penalty term, the gradient calculation, and the weight update step.\n",
    "import numpy as np\n",
    "\n",
    "# ----- Setup -----\n",
    "x = 0.5\n",
    "w = 0.8\n",
    "b = 0.2\n",
    "t = 1.0   # target output\n",
    "eta = 0.1 # learning rate\n",
    "lmbda = 0.01 # weight decay coefficient\n",
    "\n",
    "# ----- Forward pass -----\n",
    "z = w * x + b\n",
    "y = 1 / (1 + np.exp(-z))   # sigmoid activation\n",
    "L_original = 0.5 * (y - t)**2\n",
    "\n",
    "# ----- Add L2 regularization -----\n",
    "L_total = L_original + lmbda * (w**2)\n",
    "\n",
    "# ----- Backpropagation terms -----\n",
    "# Gradient of original loss wrt weight\n",
    "dLorig_dw = (y - t) * y * (1 - y) * x\n",
    "\n",
    "# Gradient of regularization term wrt weight\n",
    "dLreg_dw = 2 * lmbda * w\n",
    "\n",
    "# Total gradient\n",
    "dLtotal_dw = dLorig_dw + dLreg_dw\n",
    "\n",
    "# ----- Weight update -----\n",
    "w_new = w - eta * dLtotal_dw\n",
    "\n",
    "# ----- Print results -----\n",
    "print(f\"z = {z:.4f}\")\n",
    "print(f\"y (sigmoid output) = {y:.4f}\")\n",
    "print(f\"L_original = {L_original:.6f}\")\n",
    "print(f\"L_total (with L2) = {L_total:.6f}\")\n",
    "print(f\"dL_original/dw = {dLorig_dw:.6f}\")\n",
    "print(f\"dL_reg/dw = {dLreg_dw:.6f}\")\n",
    "print(f\"dL_total/dw = {dLtotal_dw:.6f}\")\n",
    "print(f\"Updated weight w_new = {w_new:.6f}\")\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "🧾 What this shows\n",
    "- Original loss: L_{\\mathrm{original}}=0.5(y-t)^2\n",
    "- Total loss: L_{\\mathrm{total}}=L_{\\mathrm{original}}+\\lambda w^2\n",
    "- Gradient:\n",
    "\\frac{\\partial L_{\\mathrm{total}}}{\\partial w}=\\frac{\\partial L_{\\mathrm{original}}}{\\partial w}+2\\lambda w- Update rule:\n",
    "w\\leftarrow w-\\eta \\cdot \\frac{\\partial L_{\\mathrm{total}}}{\\partial w}\n",
    "👉 Run this and you’ll see how the extra 2\\lambda w term nudges the weight downward, even if the data gradient is small. That’s the “decay” effect.\n",
    "\n",
    "Would you like me to extend this snippet to compare updates with vs without L2 side‑by‑side, so you can see numerically how much shrinkage regularization adds?\n"
   ]
  }
 ],
